      "text": "Calculus\n\n-> Calculus is a branch of mathematics that studies continuous change and motion, allowing for\n    analysis of variables over time or other variables.\n-> It deals with infinitesimal changes of values.\n\n\nLimit:\n\n-> Limit answers the following question:\n    Where is the output y = f(x) approaching as the input x gets infinitely close to a target point a?\n-> This means a limit never asks about what happens at x = a, it only cares about the trend as you \n    approach a from immediate neighborhood.\n-> So, we cannot get a limit at x=a, using f(a). The value that f(x) approaches as x approaches a might be\n    different from the value defined at x = a. In some cases, the function itself might be not defined at x = a.\n-> eg: x^2 - 1 / x - 1 is not defined at x = 1. But Limit when x tends to 1, exists for this function. It is 2.\n\n-> We say limit exists for x at a, iff the value f(x) approaches when x approaches a from left(i.e. a-h to a) and the\n    the value f(x) approaches when x approaches a from the right(i.e. a+h to a) are EQUAL.\n-> So, Limit exists for f(x) at a, only when LHL and RHL are same at a. If not, we say Limit DOES NOT EXIST(DNE)\n\n\nFour Reasons Limits fail to exist:\n1) Jump Discontinuities (LHL != RHL)\n    - Function approaches finite numbers from both sides, but they do not match.\n    - Happens in piece wise functions like floor/ceil of x.\n\n2) Infinte Behavior / Vertical Asymptotes\n    - Function shoots upto +INF or drops down to -INF as you get closer to a.\n    - Happens when there is divison by zero while numerator exisits. eg: 1 / x - 3 at x = 3.\n    - It might happen that both LHL and RHL are equal to +INF. But this case is considered as \n      non-existence of limit even though LHL and RHL match.\n\n3) Infinte Oscillation\n    - Function oscillates faster and faster between two bounds as it gets closer to a.\n    - Functions like f(x) = sin(1/x), near x = 0\n    - As x -> 0, the input 1/x -> INF causing sine to cycle infintely between 1 and -1 within any \n      tiny window around 0. No single target L can be pinned down.\n\n4) Boundary Points of Limited Domains\n    - Function is not defined on both sides of x = a.\n    - eg: sqrt(x) is not defined for negative numbers so LHL at 0 doesn't exist.\n\n\nIndeterminate Forms:\n\n-> An indeterminate form is an algebraic expression that is obtained when evaluating a limit\n    by directly substituting x = a which doesn't give enough information to determine the limit's value.\n-> This doesn't mean limit DNE, it just requires more work to determine if limit exists or not.\n-> Notice, that we are dealing with changes that too infinitesimal changes. 0 is not just zero. INF is not\n    just infinity. one zero can be larger than another zero and one infinity can be larger than another infinity.\n\nThere are 7 Indeterminate forms:\n\n1) 0 * INF\n    - 0 can be decaying faster than INF is growing, in that case, the result will be zero.\n    - 0 can be decaying slower than INF is growing, in that case, the result will be INF.\n    - Signs don't affect the determinability. They just change the sign of result. Based on the operation.\n      In this case, it is multiplicaton. Always treat as if the sign is embedded inside the variables.\n    - If the rates were identical, the two terms would cancel out and such terms are determinable.\n      They leave a constant behind. Same can be said about any operation.\n    - Last two points are not applicable for power operation.\n\n2) 0/0\n    - If numerator 0 decays faster than denominator 0, the result will be zero.\n    - If numerator 0 decays slower than numerator 0, the result will be INF.\n\n3) INF/INF\n    - If numerator INF grows faster than denominator INF, the result will be INF.\n    - If numerator INF grows slower than denominator INF, the result will be 0.\n\n4) INF - INF\n    - If first INF is growing faster than second INF, the result will be +ve INF.\n    - If first INF is growing slower than second INF, hte result will be -ve INF.\n  \n5) 0 ^ 0\n    - If base is negative(i.e. approaching 0 from left), then limit doesn't exist.\n    - If base is +ve and power is positive, the result will be either 0 or 1 based on the decay rates.\n    - If base is +ve and power is negative, the result will be either INF or 1 based on the decay rates.\n\n6) 1 ^ INF\n    -> case 1: 1 approaching from right\n        - If decay of 1 is faster than INF grows, the result is 1.\n        - If decay of 1 is slower than INF grows, the result is INF.\n    -> case 2: 1 approaching from left\n        - If growth of 1 is faster than INF, the result is 1.\n        - If growth of 1 is slower than INF, the result is 0.\n    -> If INf were negative, the results would be 1/result. for each case.\n\n7) INF ^ 0\n    -> Case 1: +ve INF and +ve 0\n        - If growth of INF is faster than rate of decay of 0, result is INF\n        - If growth of INF is slower than rate of decay of 0, result is 1\n    -> case 2: +ve INF and -ve 0\n        - if growth of INF is faster than rate of decay of 0, result is 0\n        - if growth of INF is slower than te reate of decay of 0, result is 1\n    -> case 3: -ve INF adn +ve 0\n        - this is undefined.\n    -> case 4: -ve INF and -ve 0\n        - this is undefined.\n\n\n-> Methods to determine:\n    0/0 - Factorization, Rationalization, L'Hôpital's Rule\n    INF/INF- Dividing by highest power, L'Hôpital's Rule\n    0 * INF - Rewrite as 0/0 or INF/INF\n    INF - INF - Rationalize or Combine Fractions and Factor out Dominant terms\n    exponent Forms - Take natural log, the result is e power lim x->a g(x) ln(f(x)). \n        This will be in one of the preceeding forms and can be handled.\n\nL'Hôpital's Rule:\n- If the limit x -> a (f(x)/g(x)) results in either 0/0 or INF/INF, then result is lim x-> a (f'(x)/g'(x))\n\n\nContinuity:\n\n-> Intuitively, a function is continuous at a point if the graph can be drawn through the point without lifting your pencil.\n    i.e. there are no holes or jumps in the graph at that point and there is no infinte asymptote, i.e. function is defined at that point.\n-> Formally, f(x) is continuous at a, iff LHL = RHL = f(a).\n\n\nFormulae involving Limits and Continuity:\n\n->\n\n\n\nDifferentiability:\n\n-> Limits ask where the function is heading. Continuity asks if the graph is connected without breaks.\n    Differentiability asks if the graph is smooth enough to have a well-defined direction (slope) at every single point.\n-> If you zoom in extremely close to a curve that is differentiable at a point, it stops looking like\n    a curve and begins to look like a straight line. This concept is called LOCAL LINEARITY.\n-> The derivative at point x = a, written as f'(a), is simply the slope of that tanget line:\n    slope m = change if y / change in x. In other words it is the rate of change in y with respect to \n    change in x.\n-> f'(a) = lim h -> 0 f(a + h) - f(a) / h. f'(a) to exist limit must exist as a finite real number. which means\n    LHD must be equal to RHD.\n\n\n-> So, if you were to see derivatives outside the graphical world. Take the example of position of\n    a car with respect to time.\n-> The derivative at any point of time, measures the rate of change of position of the car with respect to\n    change in time. This is nothing but the velocity. That too the instantaneous velocity.\n\n4 ways differentiability fails:\n\n- Discontinuity\n- Sharp Corners (LHD != RHD). Direction changes instantly. No matter how much you zoom in it will never look like straight line.\n- Vertical Tangent lines (Infinite slope). For an instant the rate of growth is unbounded\n- Wild oscillations in slope.\n\n\nProperties of Plot of f'(x):\n\n- f'(x) plots the slope of f(x) at each point.\n- if f'(x) > 0 at a point, then that means the rate of change of y w.r.t x is positive. Y is increasing as X is increasing.\n- if f'(x) < 0 at a point, then slope is negative, meaning f(x) is behaving like a decreasing function at this point.\n- if f'(x) = 0, then that means f(x) has momentarily became a straight line at some point. meaning, rate of change is zero.\n\n\nMaxima and Minima. Collectively known as Exterma. They are values of y.\n\n-> Maxima occurs at a peak, Minima occurs at a trench.\n-> Local vs Global: The maximum/minimum of all local maxima/local minima is Global Maxima/Global Minima AND the values at end points.\n\n-> It is common sense to notice that, a maxima or minima occurs, when there is a change in sign of slope after a momentarily point\n    when slope become zero. These points are called Critical Points and are the candidates for positions where extrema can occur.\n-> All you need to do to find the critical points is solve, f'(x) = 0.\n\nHow to find the Extrema?\nThe First Derivative Test:\n    1) Find Critical Points\n    2) For each CP, test points just to the left and just to the right, to check the sign of the slope.\n    3) Classify the point based on the direction of the slope.\n\n-> + to - is Peak, Local Maxima\n-> - to + is Trench, Local Minima\n-> Neither, a candidate for INFLECTION point.\n\n\nSecond Derivative (or the Double Derivative):\n\n-> First derivative, finds the rate of change of y w.r.t x.\n-> Second derivaite, finds the rate of change of (rate of change of y w.r.t x) w.r.t x.\n-> That is, Second derivative finds the rate of change of slope of f(x).\n\n-> If f(x) gives position with respect to time, f'(x) gives velocity w.r.t time, f''(x) gives acceleratin w.r.t time.\n\n\nWhat does it mean when f''(x) = 0?\n\n-> it means the rate of change of slope is zero at that instant, i.e. f'(x) = c. That's it. it doesn't tell anything about\n    f(x) itself. Whether it is increasing or decreasing.\n\n-> But along with f''(x) = 0, if you have\n    - f'(x) > 0 : Then that means f(x) is increasing at that instant.\n    - f'(x) < 0 : Then that means f(x) is decreasing at that instant.\n\n-> f'(x) = 0, gives direct information about f(x) that f(x) is not changing at all, rate of change is zero.\n-> Combining the values of f''(x) with f'(x) = 0, gives useful information:\n    - f''(x) > 0 : this says that near that infinitesimally small point, i can see an increase in slope.\n                    Now, we can never reach this point with a decrease in slope. Because that would cause the\n                    the function to be non-differentiable at the point. so it must be increasing. There by forming a\n                    local minima.\n    - f''(x) < 0: same explanation. forms a local maxima\n    - f''(x) = 0: Check around that point, if the sign of f''(x) changes remain -ve on both sides, Then it is Maxima.\n                   If it remains +ve on both side, it is minima. \n                   If it changes from -ve to +ve, Inflection point, Forms mirror of Integral symbol, Concave down to up\n                   If it changes from +ve to -ve, Inflection point, Forms Integral symbol, Concave up to down\n \n    \n\nSecond Derivative Test:\n    1) Find Critical Points.\n    2) Substitute them in f''(x). If f''(x) > 0, minima. If f''(x) < 0, maxima. \n    3) If f''(x) = 0, fallback to the First derivative test. (Finding the signs of f''(x) to the left and right is error prone\n        coz, we don't know haw far to the left or right would be a good length to find the symbols.)\n\n\n\nALL FORMULAE AND RULES AND PROPERTIES OF DIFFERENTIATION\n\n\nIntermediate Value Theorem:\n\n-> In simple terms it states that a continuous curve cannot jump from one height to another without passing\n    through every single height in between.\n-> If f(x) is continuous on the closed interval [a, b], and N is any target value strictly between f(a) and f(b)\n    Then there must exist at least one number c in the open interval (a, b) such that f(c) = N\n\n\nMean Value Theorem:\n\n-> If your average velocity in a stretch of time is S, then there must exist atleast one moment in that stretch, where your\n    instantaneous velocity is equal to average velocity\n-> If f(x) is continuous on the [a, b] and differentiable on (a, b), Then there must exist atleast one number c in (a, b),\n    such that the instantaneous slope equals the average slope.\n    \n    f'(c) = f(b) - f(a) / b - a\n\n\nRolle's Theorem:\n\n-> If you started your journery at point A and returned to point A, Then there must exist atleast one point in the journey\n    where your velocity was momentarily zero.\n-> If f(x) is continuous on [a, b], differentiable on (a, b) and f(a) = f(b), then there must exist atleast one number c in (a, b)\n    such that, f'(c) = 0.\n\n\n-> If f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs and f(x) is continous on [a, b], then number of roots between (a, b) is always odd.\n-> If f(a) and f(b) have same signs and f(x) is continuous on [a, b], then number of roots between (a, b) is always even.\n\n\n\n\nIntegration:\n\n-> Integration is the opposite of differentiation. Differentiation splits the function into infinitesimal instants and\n    analyses them. Whereas, Integration adds up the tiny changes. It accumulates CHANGES\n\n-> If you integrate the velocity function f(t), you will get the Position Function F(t), which gives accumulated changes in position.\n    So if you were to find out the total change in position from t1 to t2. You do F(t2) - F(t1).\n\n-> Notice that we are accumulating CHANGES. So, if t1 were not the starting position, we wouldn't actually know\n    where the position is at t2, we only know \"the magnitude of the change\", to find out it's position, we need to \n    add the change to the f(t1).\n\n-> But, if t1 is the starting point, then it shall give the position at t2.\n\n\n-> Integration function is actually calculating the area under the curve of velocity function, f(t).\n    Integrate from a to b ( f(t) . dt ). If you break down this formula:\n    - f(t) represents velocity at a point, (which is the height) and dt represents the tiny change in t (which is width).\n    - Multiplying these two gives the area of tiny rectangle from t to dt. The Integration symbol just sums up these\n      tiny areas to give the total area under the curve.\n\n-> So, If you take derivative of F(t) you get height at t, which is the velocity at t, plotting this gives you velocity function f(t).\n\n-> Derivative decreases the power by 1 and Integration increases the power by 1. Integration is the Anti-Derivative.\n\n-> When you just integrate a function, you get another function called the Indefinite Integral.\n-> If you want to calculate the area or the accumulate changes from a to b, you substitute a and b, in F(b) - F(a)\n    The result is called Definite Integral.\n\n\nINTEGRATION FORMULAE, TEHCHIQUES, RULES AND PROPERTIES",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
